bby  Krieger and Kevin Dillon as John  Densmore the number for a movie on our  list is a legendary group out of  Southern California as well but for  vastly different reasons 2015's Straight  Outta Compton from director F gary Gray  chronicles the formation of NWA the  gangster rap pioneers from the early 90s  the group's membership includes do see  DJ yella MC Ren and three MCS who will  go on to achieve global fame and dr. Dre  Ice Cube and eazy-e Gray does not shy  away from highlighting the past of the  group members but he also showcases the  very real societal pressures  motivated the greatest music he includes  some pulsating concert scenes as well  that bring the best of nwaz music to  life but he also finds time to highlight  the smaller more personal moments  between the members that solidified them  as a family it's so well done that when  the divisions start to show within the  group that will eventually doom them you  can't help but feel like desperate hope  for them to work it out before the  powerful loss that comes at the end  Corey Hawkins stars as dr. Dre O'Shea  Jackson jr. as Ice Cube  who is his real-life dad Jason Mitchell  is easy and Paul Giamatti as their  allegedly duplicitous manager Jerry  Heller NWA was one step ahead of the rap  game as the genre was moving away from  the hand-waving crowd leaders of the 80s  to the more brutally honest and socially  conscious music that was catching the  attention of the pop culture zeitgeist  and great perfectly captures it here ray  NAB's our number three spot Jamie Foxx's  transformation into Ray Charles is  nothing short of revelatory and he won  the Best Actor in 2004 for his  performance Taylor Hackford who was also  nominated for Best Director directs this  biopic that chronicles Charles's life  from a child who slowly goes blind at  the age of seven in the deep south to  the worldwide sensation he becomes  thanks to his unique approach to rhythm  and blues music along the way we see  Charles deal with a myriad of issues as  he climbs the ladder of Fame including  racism mistreatment from bam mates  because of his blindness anger within  his own community for using gospel music  as the foundation for his R&amp;B sound and  resistance from music executives to take  charge of the direction of his career  but what really sets this biopic apart  is the gutsiness to not show an  altogether flattering portrait of the  man as he pursues his musical dreams  Hackford and Fox show us Charles's drug  addiction in all its sadness and  ugliness they also show us his  womanizing ways and how both of these  issues threatened to destroy his  marriage and his family but overall the  film is a portrait of a self-made man  who overcame his blindness to find his  unique sound through trial and error and  establish himself as a legend in the  world of music our penultimate film is  the 1984 Oscar winner for Best Picture  dais the film directed by the late Milos  Forman traces the story of Wolfgang  Amadeus Mozart through the eyes of his  greatest adversary Antonio Salieri at  least Salieri thinks he is Mozart's  greatest adversary the film is based on  Peter Schaffer's stage play and stars  Thomas Holtz as Mozart and F Murray  Abraham  it's a unique and very unstuff e  portrayal of a classical musical legend  who many consider to be the greatest  composer ever halts portrays him as a  giggling ambitious manchild who's never  fully grown up and is constantly being  saved from true disaster because of his  abilities in the end though the  pressures from his domineering stage dad  his wife Constance and her desperate  family and the manipulations of Salieri  conspire in the film to eventually break  Mozart and lead to a tragic ending along  the way however Foreman treats us to an  incredible unique biopic that delivers a  new appreciation of the man's music and  Abraham treats us to a delightfully and  frightfully unsettling portrayal of one  of the most unique villains in film  